--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Subset
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is NOT all of uriparser. zuhttp vendors the parse / resolve / recompose
closure only, which is the minimum that satisfies src/zu_uri.h:

    UriParse.c  UriParseBase.c      parse a URI reference
    UriResolve.c                    resolve a relative ref against a base (§19.5)
    UriRecompose.c                  serialise back to text
    UriCommon.c  UriMemory.c        shared helpers, memory manager
    UriIp4.c  UriIp4Base.c          IPv4 literal parsing (required by UriParse.c)

The closure was determined by linking, not by reading includes: build the four
files the design needs and add exactly what the linker reports missing, until
it links. tools/update-uriparser re-derives it and fails if it changes.

DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED, and why it matters:

    UriNormalize.c  UriNormalizeBase.c   uriNormalizeSyntax*, uriMakeOwner*
    UriQuery.c                           uriComposeQuery*, query dicts
    UriEscape.c                          uriEscape*, uriUnescape*
    UriCompare.c  UriShorten.c  UriFile.c

Six of the eight CVEs in uriparser's history are in that excluded set:

    CVE-2024-34402, CVE-2024-34403      ComposeQuery*      UriQuery.c
    CVE-2018-19198, CVE-2018-19199      ComposeQuery*      UriQuery.c
    CVE-2021-46141, CVE-2021-46142      NormalizeSyntax*   UriNormalize.c

    CVE-2018-19200                      ResetUri*          UriCommon.c   KEPT
    CVE-2018-20721                      Parse*Ex* (IPv6)   UriParse.c    KEPT

That is not luck. The excluded modules BUILD strings — they allocate, size and
concatenate — while the retained ones mostly SCAN a caller-owned buffer and
record ranges into it. Allocation arithmetic is where the overflow bugs were.

zuhttp does not need any excluded function. Case normalisation happens in
zu_uri.c against our own flat struct, and we deliberately do NOT normalise
percent-encoding in the path: rewriting a request target can change what the
origin server resolves, and neither curl nor httr2 does it either.

Uri.h still DECLARES the excluded functions. Calling one is a link error, not
a compile error. ctest links statically on all three CI platforms, so it is
caught at build time; tools/update-uriparser also greps for them.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Required build flags
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

    -DURI_NO_UNICODE      ANSI only. Drops the wchar_t half of every source
                          file (each one #includes itself twice) and with it
                          the <wchar.h> dependency. Halves the object code.
    -DURI_STATIC_BUILD    Only consulted under _MSC_VER; harmless elsewhere.
                          R builds Windows packages with mingw-w64, where
                          URI_PUBLIC is empty regardless, but set it anyway so
                          an MSVC build cannot pick up __declspec(dllimport).

src/zu_uriparser.h sets both before including <uriparser/Uri.h>, so zuhttp's
own translation units are correct even if a build file forgets the flags.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Behaviour notes that cost time to discover
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. uriParseSingleUriExMmA() REJECTS a NULL afterLast with URI_ERROR_NULL,
   even though Uri.h documents "can be NULL (to use first + strlen(first))"
   on the Ex functions. That convenience is implemented only in the non-Mm
   wrapper uriParseSingleUriEx(), which fills afterLast in before delegating.
   zuhttp always passes an explicit end pointer, which is what we want anyway:
   a Location header is a byte range, not a C string.

2. uriCompleteMemoryManager(m, backend) needs TWO DISTINCT structs. It stores
   `backend` in m->userData and installs decorating functions that call
   through it, so passing one struct as both arguments makes uriDecorateMalloc
   recurse into itself until the stack dies. zuhttp does not call it: zu_alloc
   already size-headers its blocks, so we fill all five slots directly and use
   the library's uriEmulateReallocarray for the fifth.

3. The parser is RFC 3986, not WHATWG URL. It accepts things an HTTP client
   must still reject — a port of 65536, a host of "example.com..". zu_uri.c
   applies that validation layer; see its header comment for the full list.
   It is correspondingly stricter than a browser in useful ways: a backslash,
   a space, a tab or a newline in the authority is a syntax error.
